      "englishText": "Aprastutaprasanga: Respected Avadhānī, why do you express everything through feminine gender? Has my sister (the Avadhānī's wife) appeared before your mind?\n\nAvadhānī: If your sister comes to my mind there is no harm, —but if anyone else does, then it may create problems.\n\nAprastutaprasanga: I shall inform her then, if there is anyone else.\n\nAvadhānī: (quotes from the scriptures)\n\nprDtah dyutaprasangena madhyDhne striprasangatah/\nrDtrau cauraprasangena kDlo gacchati dhimatDm//\n(The wise spend their mornings in chatting about the game of dice, the topic of woman is\n\ntheirs at noon, at night they indulge in talks about thieves)19\n\nThis is how it is said in Sanskrit. Since our programme has started at noontime, the topic 'woman' is still continuing. \n\nVarṇanā: Indeed, Poetry is your second wife.\n\nAvadhānī: (completes now the last pāda of the Eshu)\n\n...sapadi mahita jhancchD vyDkulDh smDraniyDh//\n\n(suddenly grew victim to destruction through the storm's might. This is worth-remembering.)\n\n19Traditionally in India Mahabharata is read in the morning, the Ramayana, in the noon and the Bhagavata, in the night. The game of dice here refers to Mahabharata. Topic of woman refers to Ramayana. Talks about a thief refers to Bhagavata.",
      "sanskritDevanagari": "अप्रस्तुतप्रसङ्गः: अवधानिवर्य! किं सर्वदा श्रीप्रसङ्गः एव प्रचलति । किं मम भगिनी अत्र स्मृतिपथमारोहति वा ।\n\nअवधानी: भगिनी स्मृतिपथमारोहति तर्हि चिन्ता न भवति । किन्तु भवतः अन्यः स्मृतिपथमाराहति तर्हि--- कष्टम् ।\n\nअप्रस्तुतप्रसङ्गः: तर्हि अन्या अपि अिस्त तर्हि सूचियष्यामि ।\n\nअवधानी: प्रातः द्यूतप्रसङ्गेन मध्याह्ने स्त्रीप्रसङ्गतः ।\nरात्रौ चौरप्रसङ्गेन कालो गच्छति धीमताम् ॥\nइति एकमाभाणकमिस्त । अतः रामस्य चरितमेव रामायाः चरितम् । अतः मध्याह्ने प्रारब्धमिति वत्वा सा एव अनुवर्तते । न समाप्त इति ।\n\nवर्णना: भवतामपरा श्री कविता एव ।\n\nअवधानी: ---सपदि महितण्च्छछाल्याकुलाः स्मारणीयाः ।",
